(7120605, 1086761, 50695, '<td valign="top">Lovely how the street fades to blankness. the bus is only just entering the frame... we can''t see its wheels so it appears to float. <br/> <br/>The soul is just water on asphalt, bleak but composed and presented in such a lovely way. I have a feeling I like this photo more than I would like the book. I''m sure it''s better than the musical, at least! 9<br/> <br/>I''m bumping this to 10 and putting it in my art gallery. To continue the exposition, I hang things in my gallery if I feel they are independent works of art. For example, I could publish this in a literary magazine and it would fit right in.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1077279" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Lovely how the street fades to blankness. the bus is only just entering the frame... we can''t see its wheels so it appears to float.  The soul is just water on asphalt, bleak but composed and presented in such a lovely way. I have a feeling I like this photo more than I would like the book. I''m sure it''s better than the musical, at least! 9 I''m bumping this to 10 and putting it in my art gallery. To continue the exposition, I hang things in my gallery if I feel they are independent works of art. For example, I could publish this in a literary magazine and it would fit right in. ', '2013-10-22 22:57:39.000000', NULL, 1),
(7120606, 1086679, 50695, '<td valign="top">This is well put together. I resist the icon in the middle of an otherwise stunningly ambiguous scene of sun worship... but it does give a bizarre sense of collage. Besides, you clearly know what you''re doing. I trust you. 9<br/> <br/> <br/>I''m bumping this to 10 and putting it in my art gallery. To continue the exposition, I hang things in my gallery if I feel they are independent works of art. For example, I could publish this in a literary magazine and it would fit right in.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1077279" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg"/></a></td>', 'This is well put together. I resist the icon in the middle of an otherwise stunningly ambiguous scene of sun worship... but it does give a bizarre sense of collage. Besides, you clearly know what you''re doing. I trust you. 9  I''m bumping this to 10 and putting it in my art gallery. To continue the exposition, I hang things in my gallery if I feel they are independent works of art. For example, I could publish this in a literary magazine and it would fit right in. ', '2013-10-22 22:57:54.000000', NULL, 1),
(7120607, 1087039, 50695, '<td valign="top">I don''t know the book but this certainly evokes the title. I quite like the big area of nothingness in the upper left and how you even tilted the horizon to get this phenomenon. Is this some sort of worship of the great invisible aboveness? It is certainly the primary light source of the image, a light source that your processing emphasizes to stunning effect. 8<br/> <br/> <br/>I''m bumping this to 10 and putting it in my art gallery. To continue the exposition, I hang things in my gallery if I feel they are independent works of art. For example, I could publish this in a literary magazine and it would fit right in.<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1077279" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg"/></a></td>', 'I don''t know the book but this certainly evokes the title. I quite like the big area of nothingness in the upper left and how you even tilted the horizon to get this phenomenon. Is this some sort of worship of the great invisible aboveness? It is certainly the primary light source of the image, a light source that your processing emphasizes to stunning effect. 8  I''m bumping this to 10 and putting it in my art gallery. To continue the exposition, I hang things in my gallery if I feel they are independent works of art. For example, I could publish this in a literary magazine and it would fit right in. ', '2013-10-22 22:58:05.000000', NULL, 1),
